# Token optimization

Use the local knowledge graph at `graphify-out/` before exploring code. A scoped query is a few thousand tokens; reading files, `graph.json`, `GRAPH_REPORT.md`, or the Obsidian vault is not.

Run `graphify` from `~/.local/bin` (v0.9.68). If the command is missing, prefix `export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"`.

Before Read, Grep, Glob, or a search Bash for a codebase question, run one of:

- `graphify query "<question>"` — scoped subgraph
- `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` — how two symbols connect
- `graphify explain "<symbol>"` — one node and its neighbors

Pass the same instruction to every subagent that explores code.

Read a file only after the graph names it and you need those lines to edit or debug, or when `graphify-out/graph.json` is missing.

Do not load these into context:

- `graphify-out/graph.json` and `graphify-out/cache/`
- `graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md`, except a short architecture pass when query, path, and explain are not enough
- `graphify-out/obsidian/` — that vault is for the Obsidian app, not for the agent

After you change code, run `graphify update .` (local AST, no API cost). Do not run `graphify extract` without `--code-only`, or `graphify label`, unless the user asks. Those send text to an LLM.

Job-scoring payloads still go through `core/token_optimizer.py` (`minify_job`, `cache_block`). That path is separate from this graph.
